description:
The SMART-scale consists of a five-point grading scale that assesses residual flow within the aneurysm in the venous phase, after treatment with a flow diverter [Grade 0 = Any aneurysm with an early phase, coherent inflow jet; Grade 1 = Completely patent aneurysm with diffuse inflow; Grade 2 = Reduced but residual filling which reaches the aneurysm dome; Grade 3 = Saccular: residual neck filling or former aneurysm boundaries are covered;
Grade 4 = Complete occlusion of the aneurysm]. For Grade 0, a grade-modifier factor (grades 0a–0c) is used to provide consideration of the duration of stasis [Grade 0a: No relevant stasis
Grade 0b: Stasis remains into the capillary phase; Grade 0c: Stasis remains into the venous phase]. An additional a five-point scale
Is used to assess the severity of in-stent stenosis (ISS), which is the
partial or even complete occlusion of the parent artery that may occur because of the stent [ISS Grade 0 = No ISS; ISS grade 1 = Mild; ISS grade 2 = Moderate, 50–70%; ISS grade 3 = Severe;
ISS grade 4 = Occlusion].
